<title>USA: Cooler air &quot;not a threat&quot; for Brazil coffee - WSC.</title>
<headline>Cooler air &quot;not a threat&quot; for Brazil coffee - WSC.</headline>
<dateline>LEXINGTON, Mass. 1997-08-05</dateline>
<text>
<p>Cooler air continues to remain to the south of Brazil's coffee belt and does not appear strong enough to cause any damaging cold as it moves toward the region, Weather Services Corp said in an update on Tuesday.</p>
<p>Clouds and a few light showers were reported in main growing areas of the belt this morning. Temperatures were in the upper 50's to low 60's F (middle teens C).</p>
<p>Scattered showers will continue throughout northern areas of the region until Thursday. Mostly dry conditions are expected in Sao Paulo and Minas Gerais. Low temperatures should remain in the 50's to low 60's F (low to middle teens C).</p>
<p>The extended outlook calls for partly to variably cloudy skies through Saturday with light showers favoring southern regions. Temperatures may trend cooler and could reach the low to middle 50's F (low teens C). There is still no damaging cold indicated, according to WSC.</p>
<p>((Eileen Houlihan, New York Commodity Desk, 212-859-1640))</p>
